/*! \page developer_backend_complex_page Complex Numbers

\ref developer_backend_math_page     (Prev) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_backend_field_page (Next) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
<BR>

Some mathematical operations performed by PSCF require manipulations 
of complex numbers, and storage of arrays of complex numbers. The 
use of complex numbers arises in large part from the use of Fourier 
transforms in the psuedo-spectral solution of the MDE, since the 
discrete Fourier transform of a real-valued field defined on an array 
yields an array of complex-valued numbers. 

\section developer_backend_complex_types_sec Complex Number Types

Throughout the PSCF source code, template specializations that use
the CUDA and C++ computational backends use different data structures 
to store the real and imaginary parts of a single complex number. 
This difference is a result of the fact that these different backends 
rely on different external libraries to perform fast Fourier 
transform (FFT) operations, and these different FFT libraries use 
different representations complex numbers. 

The C++ backend uses the external FFTW library to perform FFT 
operations on a CPU, using a version that is compiled to use double 
precision real numbers.  The FFTW library uses a data type named 
fftw_complex to represent a complex number. This typename is defined 
within the fftw3.h header file for FFTW as an alias for an array 
containing two double precision numbers, representing the real and 
imaginary parts of a complex number.

The CUDA backend uses the cufft library that is provided as part 
of NVIDIA CUDA toolkit to perform FFT operations on a GPU. 
The cufft library can perform transforms using either single or
double precision for real numbers, but PSCF uses double precision 
by default. The types used by cufft for real and complex numbers
are referred to throughout the PSCF source code by typename 
aliases cudaReal and cudaComplex that are defined in the file
src/pscf/backend/cuda/cudaTypes.h. The cudaComplex type is an alias
for a struct with fields named x and y that represent real and 
imaginary parts of a complex number, respectively.

\section developer_backend_complex_aliases_sec Aliases in Backend Identifier Classes

Each of the backend identifer classes Pscf::CPT and Pscf::CUT 
container definitions of a type aliases named Real and Complex
that are aliases for the types used for real and complex numbers 
by the FFT library used by the associated backend. 


The definition of class Pscf::CPT thus reads, in part
\code
namespace Pscf {

    class CPT {
       
        static const BackendId id = BackendId::Cpp;

        using Real = double;
        using Complex = fftw_complex;

        // ...
        
    };

}
\endcode
Here fftw_complex is the complex number type used by the FFTW
fast Fourier transform library.

The corresponding definition of Pscf::CUT reads, in part
\code
namespace Pscf {

    class CUT {
       
        static const BackendId id = BackendId::Cuda;

        using Real = cudaReal
        using Complex = cudaComplex;

        // ...
        
    };

}
\endcode
Here, cudaReal and cudaComplex are aliases defined with the
PSCF package for the real and complex types used by the 
cufft library.

Within template code in which a template parameter T represents
a backend identifier class, the names "typename T::Real" and 
"typename T::Complex" can thus be used to refer generically to
the real and complex types used by the associated FFT class.

<BR>
